<tr id="S5.T2.1.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.1.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:0.25pt;padding-bottom:0.25pt;">DPO-PRO</th>
<td id="S5.T2.1.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:0.25pt;padding-bottom:0.25pt;">
<span id="S5.T2.1.1.6.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block" style="width:227.6pt;">
<span id="S5.T2.1.1.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_parbox ltx_align_top" style="width:227.6pt;"><span id="S5.T2.1.1.6.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">s + 3 * (12_30-3pm and NGO_registered)</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.02709v1/AAAI/figures/task_specific_winrates_noise.png" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:558/218;" width="558" height="218" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Winrates for RMAB environment under label flipping noise. We present more results in the Appendix.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.3 Comparison with Decision-Language Model ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Preference Robustness for DPO with Applications to Public Health" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the results. In zero-noise setting, vanilla-DPO outperforms robust methods. However, as noise level increases, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> outperforms vanilla DPO and DrDPO. We also perform a task-level analysis of winrates in Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Evaluation ‣ 5.2 Benchmarks on Maternal Health Data ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Preference Robustness for DPO with Applications to Public Health" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. We note that across all tasks, under the presence of noise, robust DRO methods are better than vanilla-DPO. Especially, in 5 out of 8 evaluation task categories, DPO-PRO outperforms baselines under the presence of noise.
This signifies the robustness of our approach.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To further illustrate the differences between vanilla DPO and <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span>, we present response outputs from both models on two representative tasks from the evaluation dataset (Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Evaluation ‣ 5.2 Benchmarks on Maternal Health Data ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Preference Robustness for DPO with Applications to Public Health" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Many tasks contain attributes that can be interpreted in multiple ways. For instance, the concept of “young beneficiaries” can be identified directly through the age feature, but also indirectly via lower education level or income. Similarly, a task that expresses a preference for “midday calls” could refer explicitly to the 12:30–3:00 PM time slot, but might also include the 10:30–12:30 PM window, depending on interpretation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In such cases, we observe that vanilla-DPO favors uncommon interpretations of ambiguous attributes based on spurious correlations in noisy preference data. In contrast, <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> adopts a cautious strategy by selecting narrower definition, aiming to be robust to uncertainty in the preference signal at evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Comparison with Decision-Language Model</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.3 Comparison with Decision-Language Model ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Preference Robustness for DPO with Applications to Public Health" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> compares <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> with the Decision Language Model (DLM), a self-reflection-based approach introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Behari et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, using two evaluation metrics: (1) <em id="S5.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">win rate</em>, determined by an external LLM judge (GPT-4o-mini), and (2) <em id="S5.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reward</em>, computed using ground-truth human-designed reward functions. While DLM slightly outperforms <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> on both metrics, the results indicate that <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> achieves comparable alignment quality despite its simplicity and significantly lower inference-time cost.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Preference Robustness for DPO with Applications to Public Health" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> provides detailed comparison of inference-time cost between <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> and DLM. Unlike DLM, whose inference cost scales linearly with number of arms due to repeated simulation and self-reflection, <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> incurs a constant inference cost regardless of problem size. This efficiency gap becomes increasingly significant in large-scale settings, making <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> more scalable. While overall performance is comparable or mixed across evaluation metrics, minimal inference-time overhead makes <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> especially well-suited for real-world deployment.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Winrate (%, <math id="S5.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math>) comparision for ARMMAN environment for different methods under no noise and high noise settings, aggregated across all tasks.</figcaption>
