</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by reward function design tasks in RMABs for public health, we introduced <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span>, a robust fine-tuning algorithm that enhances DPO by explicitly modeling uncertainty in preference distributions through a lightweight DRO formulation. Our method is particularly well-suited for high-stakes decision-making settings like public health, where preference annotations are inherently noisy and often limited. Compared to existing DRO-based approaches, <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> avoids excessive conservatism while providing strong theoretical justification as a regularized variant of DPO. Through extensive evaluation on a real-world maternal health program and standard alignment benchmarks, we demonstrated that <span id="S6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> improves robustness, and achieves competitive performance to inference-time methods such as self-reflection at a fraction of the computational cost. Additionally, contrary to inference-time methods, DPO-PRO removes the dependency on external LLM APIs at deployment which is essential when working with sensitive or private data.
These results highlight the potential of <span id="S6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">DPO-PRO</span> as a scalable and reliable tool for learning aligned reward functions in real-world policy learning tasks for public health.</p>
</div>
</section>
