<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Codebase</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We release the codebase for all our experiments on finetuning LLMs here: <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink ltx_url ltx_ref_self" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">https://anonymous.4open.science/r/DPO-Pro-A33D/README.md</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Extensions and Generalizations</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Beyond the Chi-squared Divergence</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use the chi-squared divergence to define the ambiguity set due to the simplicity of its resulting closed-form solution. However, alternative divergence measures can also be used and are likely to offer comparable computational efficiency as ours. This is because the per-sample optimization problem in Eq (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In 4.2 Computing Worst-case Distribution ‣ 4 DPO with Preference Robustness ‣ Preference Robustness for DPO with Applications to Public Health" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) remains a one-dimensional problem with linear objective. For example, using the KL divergence leads to a constraint of the form: <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\log\frac{p}{q}+(1-p)\log\frac{1-p}{1-q}\;\leq\;\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\log\frac{p}{q}+(1-p)\log\frac{1-p}{1-q}\;\leq\;\rho</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, the worst-case <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed by solving the equation <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\log\frac{p}{q}+(1-p)\log\frac{1-p}{1-q}\;=\;\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mi>q</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\log\frac{p}{q}+(1-p)\log\frac{1-p}{1-q}\;=\;\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be efficiently handled using a simple one-dimensional root-finding method.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Obtaining the Soft-score <math id="A2.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In many practical applications beyond ours, only a single binary annotation might be available per <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y_{1},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y_{1},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, we effectively observe only one sample from the underlying Bernoulli distribution <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(y_{1}\succ y_{2}|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(y_{1}\succ y_{2}|x)</annotation></semantics></math>, which makes estimating <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> challenging.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, our method naturally accommodates binary annotations, which can be especially useful when part of the dataset is labeled by humans. For instance, a healthcare worker involved in the annotation process may choose to directly express their preferences, in which case requesting a binary label is more appropriate. We can slightly relax the definition of chi-squared divergence by rewriting the constraint as
<math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p-q)^{2}\leq\rho\,q(1-q)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p-q)^{2}\leq\rho\,q(1-q),</annotation></semantics></math>
which reduces to <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}=q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}=q</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. As a result, when hard binary labels are used, the DRO loss reduces to the standard DPO loss, introducing no additional regularization. This effectively means that we regularize only on LLM-generated annotations, while treating human-provided labels as ground truth.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To inject robustness in such cases, we can smooth the binary label by replacing the label <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is a small hyperparameter. The DRO formulation can then be applied to this smoothed label. This makes the uncertainty-weighted coefficient uniform across all pairs (since <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> only takes values <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>), but the <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> term still encourages the model to reduce overconfidence in its current preference.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our method also applies to the case where off-the-shelf reward models are used for DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al. 2025</a>)</cite>. In this setting, acquiring soft score <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(y_{1}\succ y_{2}|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(y_{1}\succ y_{2}|x)</annotation></semantics></math> is straightforward. The scalar outputs of the reward model can be converted into a pairwise preference probability using the BT model. However, these preference probabilities can be noisy or uncalibrated. We discuss alternative annotation formats and how our approach adapts robustness to each setting in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Uncertainty-Weighted Coefficient</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We visualize this uncertainty-weighted coefficient for various <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> values in Figure <a href="#A2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ B.3 Uncertainty-Weighted Coefficient ‣ Appendix B Extensions and Generalizations ‣ Preference Robustness for DPO with Applications to Public Health" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.02709v1/AAAI/figures/penalty.png" id="A2.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:349/224;" width="349" height="224" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Visualization of the uncertainty-weighted coefficient for various values of <math id="A2.F4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>. This coefficient attains its maximum near <math id="A2.F4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> and decreases as <math id="A2.F4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A2.F4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\to 1</annotation></semantics></math>. For small values of <math id="A2.F4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, the maximum is achieved exactly at <math id="A2.F4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.F4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}</annotation></semantics></math> peaks. However, for larger <math id="A2.F4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, the maximum occurs at the intersection point between the curves <math id="A2.F4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.F4.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is because <math id="A2.F4.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q+\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q+\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}</annotation></semantics></math> (the worst-case distribution under the chi-squared divergence constraint) may exceed 1 when <math id="A2.F4.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is large, causing the worst-case distribution <math id="A2.F4.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math> to be clipped at 1. In such cases, the adversary’s perturbation <math id="A2.F4.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}-q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}-q</annotation></semantics></math> can be greater when <math id="A2.F4.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&lt;0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&lt;0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, leading the penalty term to peak at a value of <math id="A2.F4.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> smaller than 0.5.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Omitted Proofs</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Proof of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 4.1. ‣ 4.3 Efficient Optimization of the Loss ‣ 4 DPO with Preference Robustness ‣ Preference Robustness for DPO with Applications to Public Health" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a></h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The result follows from Danskin’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bertsekas 1999</a>)</cite>. To apply the theorem, we must verify that the inner maximization problem in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In 4.2 Computing Worst-case Distribution ‣ 4 DPO with Preference Robustness ‣ Preference Robustness for DPO with Applications to Public Health" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) has a compact feasible set and admits a unique optimal solution.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To establish compactness, we need to show that the feasible set is both bounded and closed. Boundedness is immediate since <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Closedness follows from the fact that the constraint function <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\mapsto(p-q)^{2}-\rho q(1-q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\mapsto(p-q)^{2}-\rho q(1-q)</annotation></semantics></math> is continous in <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, the set <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p-q)^{2}\leq\rho q(1-q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p-q)^{2}\leq\rho q(1-q)</annotation></semantics></math> is closed. Since <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is also closed and the intersection of closed sets is closed, the feasible set is closed. Uniqueness of the optimal solution <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is also straightforward, since the objective is one-dimensional linear function in <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Proof of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4.4 Analysis of the Loss Function ‣ 4 DPO with Preference Robustness ‣ Preference Robustness for DPO with Applications to Public Health" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a></h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by assuming the case <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}\geq\ell_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}\geq\ell_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,</p>
<table id="A3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{DRO}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DRO</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{DRO}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\hat{p}\,\ell_{1}+(1-\hat{p})\,\ell_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\hat{p}\,\ell_{1}+(1-\hat{p})\,\ell_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=q\,\ell_{1}+(1-q)\,\ell_{-1}+(\hat{p}-q)(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=q\,\ell_{1}+(1-q)\,\ell_{-1}+(\hat{p}-q)(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}+\underbrace{\min\left\{1-q,\;\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\}}_{\text{Uncertainty-Weighted Coefficient}}(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DPO</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Uncertainty-Weighted Coefficient</mtext></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}+\underbrace{\min\left\{1-q,\;\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\}}_{\text{Uncertainty-Weighted Coefficient}}(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For the opposite case, when <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{-1}&gt;\ell_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{-1}&gt;\ell_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the derivation proceeds symmetrically.</p>
<table id="A3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{DRO}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DRO</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{DRO}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\hat{p}\,\ell_{1}+(1-\hat{p})\,\ell_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\hat{p}\,\ell_{1}+(1-\hat{p})\,\ell_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}+(\hat{p}-q)(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DPO</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}+(\hat{p}-q)(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}-\min\left\{q,\,\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\}(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DPO</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}-\min\left\{q,\,\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\}(\ell_{1}-\ell_{-1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}+\min\left\{q,\,\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\}(\ell_{-1}-\ell_{1})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DPO</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}+\min\left\{q,\,\sqrt{\rho\,q(1-q)}\right\}(\ell_{-1}-\ell_{1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Multi-armed Restless Bandits</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A Multi-Armed Restless Bandit problem is characterized by N Markov Decision processes, each defined by the tuple <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\mathcal{R},\mathcal{T},\gamma\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\mathcal{R},\mathcal{T},\gamma\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the set of states, <math id="A4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the set of actions which are binary in <math id="A4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{S}\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\times\mathcal{S}\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> represents the transition probability from current state, under an action to the next state. <math id="A4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}:\mathcal{S}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}:\mathcal{S}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> represents a reward function mapping state to a real number, and <math id="A4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> represents the discounting factor.
We also have a budget <math id="A4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> which represents the number of arms that can be chosen at every timestep.
The objective of the Multi-Armed Restless Bandit problem then is to decide a vector of actions <math id="A4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{A}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">A</mtext><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{A}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> representing actions <math id="A4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}^{t}\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}^{t}\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> for every arm i in time step <math id="A4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> such that we maximize long term reward under the budget constraint:</p>
<table id="A4.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="J(s)=\max_{\textbf{A}\in\mathcal{A}}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N}R_{i}(s_{i})+\gamma\mathbb{E}[J(s^{\prime})\mid s,\textbf{A}]\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">A</mtext><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">A</mtext><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(s)=\max_{\textbf{A}\in\mathcal{A}}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{N}R_{i}(s_{i})+\gamma\mathbb{E}[J(s^{\prime})\mid s,\textbf{A}]\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A4.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s.t.\sum_{i=1}^{i=N}A_{i}^{t}\leq K\;\forall\;t=1\;\text{to}\;T" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">.</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></munderover><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.447em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>to</mtext><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s.t.\sum_{i=1}^{i=N}A_{i}^{t}\leq K\;\forall\;t=1\;\text{to}\;T</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s_{t}\sim T(s_{t-1},a_{t},.)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}\sim T(s_{t-1},a_{t},.)</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Experimental Details</h2>

<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Additional Comparison with Baselines</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">rDPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chowdhury, Kini, and Natarajan 2024</a>)</cite> does not use a DRO framework. Instead, it assumes that the observed preference labels are flipped at a known rate <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in[0,0.5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in[0,0.5)</annotation></semantics></math>. Under this assumption, they derive an unbiased estimator of the DPO loss by reweighting the terms as follows:</p>
<table id="A5.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{1-2\epsilon}\left[(1-\epsilon)\ell(x,y_{1},y_{2}1)-\epsilon\ell(x,y_{1},y_{2},-1)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{1-2\epsilon}\left[(1-\epsilon)\ell(x,y_{1},y_{2}1)-\epsilon\ell(x,y_{1},y_{2},-1)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the chosen response in the noisy data.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">DrDPO applies DRO over the empirical data distribution using a KL-divergence-based ambiguity set:</p>
<table id="A5.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\pi_{\theta}}\max_{P\in\mathcal{Q}(\mathcal{D})}\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)\sim P}[\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}(\pi_{\theta})]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\pi_{\theta}}\max_{P\in\mathcal{Q}(\mathcal{D})}\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)\sim P}[\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}(\pi_{\theta})],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{Q}(\mathcal{D}):=\left\{P:D_{\text{KL}}(P\|\mathcal{D})\leq\rho\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}(\mathcal{D}):=\left\{P:D_{\text{KL}}(P\|\mathcal{D})\leq\rho\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Using duality, the inner maximum (the objective to minimize) is approximated by:</p>
<table id="A5.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{\prime}\log\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)\sim\mathcal{D}}\left[\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta^{\prime}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}(\pi_{\theta})\right)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{\prime}\log\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{1},y_{2},c)\sim\mathcal{D}}\left[\exp\left(\frac{1}{\beta^{\prime}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}(\pi_{\theta})\right)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Whittle Index Policy</h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Whittle index is a heuristic that is asymptotically optimal for planning in Multi-Armed Restless Bandits.
To calculate whittle index, first we define the value function as</p>
<table id="A5.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{i}^{\lambda}(s)=\max_{a\in\{0,1\}}Q_{i}(s,a_{i},\lambda)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{i}^{\lambda}(s)=\max_{a\in\{0,1\}}Q_{i}(s,a_{i},\lambda).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}(s,a_{i},\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}(s,a_{i},\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> measures the expected discounted cumulative future reward where a reward compensation <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is added to the reward when the passive action is taken. The Whittle index associated to the state <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is then defined as:</p>
<table id="A5.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A5.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle W_{i}(s_{i}):=\inf_{m}\left\{Q_{i}(s_{i},a_{i}=0,m)=Q_{i}(s_{i},a_{i}=1,m)\right\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mi>m</mi></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W_{i}(s_{i}):=\inf_{m}\left\{Q_{i}(s_{i},a_{i}=0,m)=Q_{i}(s_{i},a_{i}=1,m)\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The Whittle Index intuitively captures the value of taking an active action on an arm. We compute the Whittle Index using a binary search algorithm proposed by <span id="A5.SS2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Qian et al. 2016.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, as a policy, we choose at every timestep the beneficiaires with top-K whittle indices.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.3 </span>ARMMAN Real World Data Attributes</h3>

<div id="A5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To conduct our experiments in the ARMMAN real-world setting, we use a dataset of 2100 beneficiaires as a subset from a quality improvement study conducted in January 2022. Specifically, we use transition probabilities of beneficiaries estimated from historical data and use these probabilities to simulate beneficiary behaviour. Additionally, for every beneficiaries, we have information in their sociodemographic attributes, registration information, as well as preference on time slots of calls and on the language in which they want to receive calls. Table <a href="#A5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ E.3 ARMMAN Real World Data Attributes ‣ Appendix E Experimental Details ‣ Preference Robustness for DPO with Applications to Public Health" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows the beneficiary attributes we use as the set of features that LLM can use in the reward function.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Features describing beneficiaries in the ARMMAN dataset</figcaption>
<table id="A5.T5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A5.T5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A5.T5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A5.T5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feature Name</span></th>
<th id="A5.T5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="A5.T5.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data Type</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A5.T5.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T5.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Enrollment gestational age</td>
<td id="A5.T5.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Int</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T5.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T5.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Enrollment delivery status</td>
<td id="A5.T5.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Int</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T5.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T5.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Gravidity (number of pregnancies)</td>
<td id="A5.T5.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Int</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T5.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T5.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Parity (number of viable pregnancies)</td>
<td id="A5.T5.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Int</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T5.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T5.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Live births count</td>
<td id="A5.T5.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Int</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T5.1.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T5.1.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Days to the first call</td>
<td id="A5.T5.1.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Int</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T5.1.8" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T5.1.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Ages 10–20</td>
<td id="A5.T5.1.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Binary</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T5.1.9" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T5.1.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Ages 21–30</td>
<td id="A5.T5.1.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Binary</td></tr>
