<section id="A5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.4 </span>RMAB Preference Data Generation</h3>

<div id="A5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To generate the preference dataset for fine-tuning an LLM, we follow these steps:</p>
<ol id="A5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a set of 190 human preference commands similar to the ones proposed in <span id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Behari et al. 2024</span>. Table  shows a smaller sample of those commands.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each of these commands, we use the DLM pipeline to generate a set of 20 relevant reward functions using prompt in Figure <a href="#A7.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Appendix G Prompts ‣ Preference Robustness for DPO with Applications to Public Health" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We sample a set of 50 pairs from these reward functions. For each of these pairs</p>
<ol id="A5.I1.i3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A5.I1.i3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">solve the RMAB problem and generate trajectories for every reward function</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">ask an LLM Judge to decide the better reward function using the prompt in Figure <a href="#A7.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Appendix G Prompts ‣ Preference Robustness for DPO with Applications to Public Health" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We perform this query 10 times to estimate LLM’s uncertainty in preference for every pair of chosen and rejected reward functions.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.5 </span>Hyperparameters</h3>

