<th id="A5.T6.1.1.27.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">learning rate scheduler</th>
<td id="A5.T6.1.1.27.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">cosine w/min_lr=1e-6</td>
<td id="A5.T6.1.1.27.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">cosine w/min_lr=1e-7</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Additional Results and Ablations</h2>

<figure id="A6.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.02709v1/AAAI/winrates_rmab_adv_noise.png" id="A6.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:349/220;" width="349" height="220" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>RMAB environment win rates under adversarial label noise</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1 </span>Adversarial Noise</h3>

<div id="A6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the main text we defined and presented results for label flipping noise. Here we consider an additional noise distribution defined as</p>
<table id="A6.Ex21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A6.Ex21X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.Ex21X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.Ex21X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\begin{cases}q-U&amp;\text{ if }q&gt;0.5\\
q+U&amp;\text{ otherwise}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext> otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\begin{cases}q-U&amp;\text{ if }q&gt;0.5\\
q+U&amp;\text{ otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A6.Ex21Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.Ex21Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.Ex21Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim\mathrm{Unif}(0,\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim\mathrm{Unif}(0,\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Similar to label flipping, adversarial noise tends to flip the labels, however, the mechanism is different as the distribution shift occurs as continuous change with an <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-ball of <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results of implementing DPO-PRO and the baselines with adversarial noise in the UltraFeedback dataset are shown in Table <a href="#A6.T7.st2" title="In Table 7 ‣ F.1 Adversarial Noise ‣ Appendix F Additional Results and Ablations ‣ Preference Robustness for DPO with Applications to Public Health" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(b)</span></a>, consistent with the trends we observed with label flipping where additional noise improves training in general, and DPO-PRO being the best performer. Figure <a href="#A6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix F Additional Results and Ablations ‣ Preference Robustness for DPO with Applications to Public Health" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows the results for the public health (RMAB) application. We show the former results in a table and the latter in a table for consistency with the main text.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.T7" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>Results on UltraFeedback under label-flip noise. Experiments run with Phi-3-mini. Mean and standard error over 2000 evaluation samples are shown.</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A6.T7.st1" class="ltx_table ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
